#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Keep a /shared store safe to expose and tidy: guard it, widen what is
left on it, expire what nobody uses, and optionally relay "this was used"
from NFS clients to the one machine whose atime counts.

    hdw4s-shared-sweep --dir DIR --idle 30m --max-age 7d
                                   one sweep pass (what the timer runs)
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --watch --dir DIR
                                   widen and drop non-files the moment they appear
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --guard DIR [--form any|i|ii]
                                   is DIR safe to walk or expose? exit 0 or 1
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --harden STORE
                                   add missing flags to a store in place, then guard
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --check --dir DIR --idle 30m [--interval 2m]
                                   the rows "hdw4s check" prints
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --relay-server --dir DIR --allow ADDR[,ADDR...]
    hdw4s-shared-sweep --relay-client --store STORE --idle 30m [--to DEST]

The manual is hdw4s-shared-sweep(8). Exit status: 0 done or accepted, 1
refused by the guard (or a red row), 2 usage or this tool is broken, 3 warnings
only (--check).

WHY A ROOT TOOL MAY WALK A TREE ANYBODY CAN CHANGE. Not because this file is
careful, although it is (every operation is relative to a directory fd and
never follows a link): because of what the GUARD requires before anything is
touched. The table is on a filesystem dedicated to it (so nothing in it is a
hard link to anything outside), mounted nosymfollow (so no path through it
follows a symlink, root's included), nodev, noexec and nosuid. The guard is
the one implementation of that rule; the expose service and "hdw4s check"
call it here rather than repeat it, because two parties writing one rule in
two languages is a defect this project has shipped twice.

NOTHING READ FROM THE TABLE OR THE WIRE IS EVER LOGGED. File names are what a
visitor chose, and a name in a persistent journal on the storage host cannot
be taken back. Counts only. The only path this tool logs is the one its
administrator configured.

"python3 -I" on the first line is load-bearing: isolated mode keeps the
working directory and PYTHONPATH off the import path, so a visitor's
table/struct.py can never be imported by a root process that happens to run
with its working directory in the table. noexec does not stop an import.
"""

import argparse
import ctypes
import errno
import ipaddress
import os
import platform
